  "title": "Un politicante da strapazzo, {cop:direbbe} il cronista",
  "translation": "A second-rate politician, the reporter would say",
  "topics": "Moving between registers, the stylistic force of word formation",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "One fact, five registers"
      },
      "Register isn't only vocabulary. Grammar, sentence length and even the choice of subject change with it.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Register",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Conversation",
              "{ip:Hanno} tagliato le tasse, finalmente!"
            ],
            [
              "Journalism",
              "Via libera al taglio delle tasse."
            ],
            [
              "Academic",
              "La riduzione della pressione fiscale {ip:risulta} riconducibile a…"
            ],
            [
              "Administrative",
              "Si {ip:dispone} la riduzione delle aliquote vigenti."
            ],
            [
              "Literary",
              "Le tasse, quell'anno, {pr:furono} più lievi."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "What moves when the register rises"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Feature",
            "Lower",
            "Higher"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Subject pronoun",
              "lui, lei",
              "egli, ella (literary)"
            ],
            [
              "Verb",
              "fare, dire, avere",
              "effettuare, affermare, disporre di"
            ],
            [
              "Noun or verb",
              "Quando {pp:è arrivato}…",
              "All'arrivo…"
            ],
            [
              "Connective",
              "però, quindi",
              "tuttavia, pertanto"
            ],
            [
              "Mood",
              "{ip:Penso} che {ip:è}…",
              "{ip:Ritengo} che {csp:sia}…"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "Mixing registers by accident sounds strange, whereas doing so on purpose can be powerful: a sudden colloquial word in a formal text can produce irony.",
      {
        "h": "Suffixes as judgements"
      },
      "Evaluative endings can state an opinion without an extra word.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Suffix",
            "Nuance",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "-ante",
              "from a neutral verb, sometimes scornful",
              "politicante, commediante"
            ],
            [
              "-astro",
              "inferior, not the real thing",
              "poetastro, giovinastro"
            ],
            [
              "-aglia",
              "contemptuous collective",
              "gentaglia, plebaglia"
            ],
            [
              "-iccio, -ognolo, -astro",
              "approximate, a bit unpleasant",
              "malaticcio, giallognolo, dolciastro"
            ],
            [
              "-uncolo, -ucolo",
              "petty",
              "omuncolo, professorucolo"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Learned formations"
      },
      "Greek and Latin elements build words in all the specialised languages: *-logia* (study), *-crazia* (power), *-fobia* (fear), *-mania* (obsession), *-cida* (killer). The prefixes *stra-* and *arci-* intensify: *strapieno*, *arcinoto*."
    ]
  },
